---
permalink: /news/feed.xml
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom">
  <title>{{ site.site_name }} - News</title>
  <subtitle>{{ site.site_desc }}</subtitle>
  <link href="{{ site.prodUrl }}/news/feed.xml" rel="self" type="application/atom+xml"/>
  <link href="{{ site.prodUrl }}/news/" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
  {%- comment -%}
    Atom's <updated> is when the feed last meaningfully changed, which is the
    newest entry — not the build time. Build time was rendered with Liquid's
    `date` filter, which formats in the build machine's local zone, then had a
    "Z" stamped after it, so a Pacific build claimed an instant 7 hours stale
    and could date the feed before its own newest entry. Using the entry also
    makes the feed byte-stable across rebuilds.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign sortedPosts = activePosts | sort: "date" | reverse %}
  {%- assign newestPost = sortedPosts | first %}
  {%- comment -%}
    Archiving every post leaves no newest entry, and an empty <updated> makes
    the whole feed unparseable rather than merely entry-less: atom:feed/updated
    is required and must be a date. The newest post overall stands in — that is
    still the last time the feed's contents meaningfully changed. `posts` reads
    committed files, so it is never itself empty.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- unless newestPost %}
    {%- assign newestPost = posts | sort: "date" | last %}
  {%- endunless %}
  <updated>{{ newestPost.date | isoDateTimeUTC }}</updated>
  <id>{{ site.prodUrl }}/news/</id>
  <author>
    <name>{{ site.site_name }}</name>
    <uri>{{ site.prodUrl }}</uri>
  </author>
  <generator>Eleventy</generator>
  {%- for post in sortedPosts %}
  <entry>
    <title>{{ post.title | escape }}</title>
    {%- comment -%}
      href is percent-encoded, <id> deliberately is not. Six published URLs
      contain a literal space (see src/_lib/post-url.js), which is not a legal
      URI character, so Atom validators reject the href — and %20 fetches the
      identical page, since that is what a browser sends for that link anyway.
      An Atom <id> is not a URL to fetch but an identity string readers dedupe
      on: re-encoding those six would re-notify every subscriber.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <link href="{{ site.prodUrl | append: post.url | append: '/' | uriPath }}" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <id>{{ site.prodUrl }}{{ post.url }}/</id>
    <published>{{ post.date | isoDateTimeUTC }}</published>
    <updated>{{ post.date | isoDateTimeUTC }}</updated>
    {%- if post.excerpt %}
    <summary>{{ post.excerpt | escape }}</summary>
    {%- endif %}
    <content type="html">{{ post.body | markdownify | escape }}</content>
    <author>
      <name>{{ site.site_name }}</name>
    </author>
    {%- for tag in post.tags %}
    <category term="{{ tag }}" />
    {%- endfor %}
  </entry>
  {%- endfor %}
</feed>
